RL: [
 {title:"Urban heat islands",text:"Today I want to talk about a phenomenon known as the urban heat island. If you have ever noticed that a city feels warmer than the surrounding countryside, especially at night, you have experienced it. The main cause is the materials we build with. Concrete, asphalt and brick absorb heat during the day and release it slowly after sunset. On top of that, cities have fewer trees and less vegetation, so there is less shade and less cooling from evaporation. Waste heat from cars, air conditioners and industry adds to the problem. The consequences are significant. Higher temperatures increase energy demand, worsen air quality and pose serious health risks, particularly for elderly people during heatwaves. So what can be done? Planners are experimenting with green roofs, reflective paving and, most simply, planting more trees. Studies suggest that increasing tree cover by even ten per cent can noticeably lower street-level temperatures.",
  model:"The lecture discussed the urban heat island effect, where cities are warmer than the surrounding countryside, especially at night. The speaker explained that concrete and asphalt absorb heat and release it slowly, while fewer trees and waste heat from cars and air conditioners make the problem worse. This leads to higher energy demand, poorer air quality and health risks during heatwaves. Finally, the lecturer suggested solutions such as green roofs, reflective paving and planting more trees."},
 {title:"The placebo effect",text:"Let's turn to one of the most fascinating puzzles in medicine: the placebo effect. A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill. Remarkably, patients who receive placebos often report real improvements, particularly for conditions like pain, nausea and anxiety. Why does this happen? Researchers believe expectation plays a central role. When we believe a treatment will work, the brain can release natural painkillers and reduce stress responses. Interestingly, the context matters too. Larger pills, more expensive treatments and even a caring conversation with a doctor can strengthen the effect. This creates a challenge for medical research. To prove that a new drug genuinely works, scientists must show that it performs better than a placebo, which is why most clinical trials compare the two in a blind design, where patients do not know which one they have received.",
  model:"The lecture was about the placebo effect, which occurs when a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill, still produces real improvements, particularly for pain, nausea and anxiety. The speaker explained that expectation is the key factor, because belief can trigger natural painkillers in the brain. Context, such as pill size, price and a caring doctor, can strengthen the effect. As a result, clinical trials compare new drugs with placebos in blind designs to prove they genuinely work."},
 {title:"How honeybees communicate",text:"How does a single honeybee tell thousands of others where to find food? The answer lies in a remarkable behaviour called the waggle dance. When a forager returns to the hive after discovering a good source of nectar, she performs a figure-of-eight movement on the vertical surface of the honeycomb. The crucial part is the straight run in the middle, during which she waggles her body from side to side. The angle of this run relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food in relation to the sun. Meanwhile, the duration of the waggle communicates distance: the longer the waggle, the further away the flowers are. Other bees follow the dancer closely, sensing these signals and the scent of the flowers she carries. This system is so precise that researchers can decode the dance and predict where bees will fly, which has made it a classic example of animal communication.",
  model:"The lecture explained how honeybees use the waggle dance to communicate the location of food. A returning forager performs a figure-of-eight movement on the honeycomb. The angle of the straight waggle run shows the direction of the food relative to the sun, while the length of the waggle indicates distance. Other bees follow the dancer and also detect the scent of the flowers. The speaker concluded that this precise system is a classic example of animal communication."}
],
