SWT: [
 {text:"The rapid rise of remote work since 2020 has prompted urban economists to reconsider the future of central business districts. For more than a century, cities were organised around a dense core where offices, shops and transport networks converged, and the daily commute was an accepted part of working life. As millions of employees discovered that many tasks could be completed from home, demand for office space in city centres fell sharply, and businesses that depended on commuter spending, such as cafés and dry cleaners, struggled to survive. However, some researchers argue that the decline of the city centre has been exaggerated. They point out that face-to-face interaction remains crucial for innovation, mentoring and building trust, and that many firms are now adopting hybrid arrangements rather than abandoning offices altogether. Furthermore, some cities are converting empty office buildings into apartments, which could bring new residents and a different kind of vitality to central areas. Rather than disappearing, therefore, city centres may be entering a period of transformation, in which they become places to live and socialise as well as to work.",
  model:"Although remote work has reduced demand for city centre offices and hurt businesses reliant on commuters, researchers argue that central business districts are not disappearing but transforming, as hybrid work continues and empty offices are converted into homes."},
 {text:"Antibiotics are among the most important medical discoveries of the twentieth century, transforming once deadly infections into easily treatable conditions. However, their effectiveness is now under serious threat. Every time antibiotics are used, bacteria that happen to carry resistance genes are more likely to survive and multiply, and over time these resistant strains can spread between people, animals and the environment. This natural process has been greatly accelerated by human behaviour. Antibiotics are frequently prescribed for viral infections, against which they have no effect, and patients often fail to complete their courses of treatment. In agriculture, large quantities are given to healthy livestock to promote growth and prevent disease in crowded conditions. The consequences are alarming: the World Health Organization regards antimicrobial resistance as one of the greatest threats to global health, and routine surgeries could become far riskier if effective antibiotics are no longer available. Addressing the problem will require coordinated action, including more careful prescribing, reduced use in farming, and greater investment in the development of new drugs, which has slowed because it offers pharmaceutical companies limited financial returns.",
  model:"Antibiotic resistance, accelerated by overprescribing, incomplete treatment and heavy use in livestock, threatens global health and routine surgery, so coordinated action is needed, including careful prescribing, reduced farm use and greater investment in new drugs."},
 {text:"The idea of a four-day working week has moved from the margins of policy debate into mainstream discussion. In several large trials conducted across Europe, North America and Australia, companies reduced working hours by roughly twenty per cent while maintaining full pay. The results were striking. Most participating organisations reported that productivity remained stable or even improved, while employees experienced lower levels of stress, burnout and sick leave. Many firms also found it easier to recruit and retain staff. Supporters argue that the traditional five-day model is a historical convention rather than an economic necessity, and that modern technology allows work to be completed more efficiently. Critics, however, caution against drawing broad conclusions. They note that the companies involved volunteered to take part and were therefore likely to be well suited to the change, and that sectors such as healthcare, hospitality and manufacturing cannot easily reduce hours without hiring additional staff. Consequently, while the evidence is encouraging, the four-day week may be more practical for some industries than for others.",
  model:"Trials of a four-day week with full pay showed stable productivity and better staff wellbeing and retention, but critics argue the volunteer companies were unrepresentative and that sectors like healthcare cannot easily cut hours, so it may suit only some industries."},
 {text:"Microplastics, fragments of plastic smaller than five millimetres, have been detected in almost every environment on Earth, from the deep ocean floor to Arctic snow and even the air in major cities. Some are manufactured deliberately, such as the tiny beads once common in cosmetics, but most result from the gradual breakdown of larger items, including bottles, packaging and synthetic clothing, which releases fibres every time it is washed. Because microplastics are so small, they are easily ingested by marine organisms and can move up the food chain, and recent studies have found them in human blood and lung tissue. Scientists do not yet fully understand the health effects, but laboratory research suggests that the particles may cause inflammation and carry harmful chemicals. Removing microplastics from the environment is extremely difficult, so most experts argue that the priority should be prevention. This includes reducing single-use plastics, improving waste management in developing countries and designing washing machines with filters that capture synthetic fibres.",
  model:"Microplastics, mostly produced by the breakdown of larger plastic items, are now found throughout the environment and in human tissue, and since their health effects are uncertain and removal is extremely difficult, experts argue that prevention through reduced plastic use and better waste management is the priority."}
],
