SST: [
 {title:"The history of the weekend",text:"Have you ever wondered where the weekend comes from? For most of history, there was no such thing. Agricultural workers laboured according to the seasons, and many religious traditions observed a single day of rest. The two-day weekend is a surprisingly recent invention. During the Industrial Revolution, factory workers often worked six days a week, with only Sunday off. Many workers, however, simply failed to turn up on Monday, a habit that became known as Saint Monday. Employers eventually negotiated a compromise: a half day on Saturday in exchange for reliable attendance on Monday. In the early twentieth century, some manufacturers went further and introduced a full two-day weekend, partly to give workers more time to spend money on consumer goods. Trade unions campaigned for the change, and by the middle of the century the five-day working week had become standard in many industrialised countries.",
  model:"The lecture explained that the two-day weekend is a recent invention. During the Industrial Revolution, factory workers had only Sunday off and often skipped Mondays, so employers offered a Saturday half day in return for attendance. Later, manufacturers and trade unions promoted a full two-day weekend, which became standard by the mid-twentieth century."},
 {title:"Why we procrastinate",text:"Procrastination is often described as a problem of time management, but psychologists increasingly see it as a problem of emotion management. When we face a task that makes us feel anxious, bored or insecure, delaying it provides immediate relief. Unfortunately, that relief is temporary, and the stress usually returns stronger as the deadline approaches. Research also shows that people tend to view their future selves almost as strangers, so it feels easier to leave difficult work for that other person to deal with. So what helps? One effective strategy is to make the first step extremely small, such as opening the document or writing a single sentence, because starting is often the hardest part. Another is self-compassion. Students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam were less likely to procrastinate before the next one, whereas those who criticised themselves harshly tended to repeat the pattern.",
  model:"The speaker argued that procrastination is mainly about managing emotions rather than time, since delaying unpleasant tasks brings short-term relief but more stress later. People also treat their future selves like strangers. Effective strategies include making the first step very small and practising self-compassion, which makes people less likely to repeat the same pattern next time."}
],
